{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Ebo8E2cDNC1sbR8g2tNtaB","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tReasoning models with chain-of-thought (CoT) can self-jailbreak: their reasoning traces transition from safe deliberation to committed harmful generation without adversarial input. We define the commitment point (CP) as the first reasoning step after which safety deliberation permanently ceases, and present a systematic empirical comparison of hidden-state (HS) vs.\\ text probes for CP detection across four reasoning LLMs (3 Qwen-family, 1 Llama) on HarmThoughts. The comparison depends critically on which transformer layer is monitored, a known sensitivity in probing methodology that we show is severe enough to qualitatively reverse conclusions in the safety domain: a crossing-rate composite metric selects L0 for R1-8B while threshold-based metrics select L14 (14-layer divergence); at 32B the divergence reaches 61 layers (exploratory$^\\dagger$), reversing whether an HS advantage is observed. Threshold-based layer selection recovers HS precision gains of +15 to +22pp at 7--8B (cross-validated via nested CV) and preliminary +15 to +19pp at 32B (exploratory$^\\dagger$, $n \\leq 20$) against a 384d sentence encoder, but this advantage is conditional on partially correlated factors: it diminishes with stronger text encoders (4/4 HB at 384d $\\to$ 1/4 at 1024d) and partially overlaps with temporal position. Seven alternative methods (change-point detection, geometric distance, representation engineering, cross-model transfer) all fail to match supervised probing. Metric-induced layer selection bias is the robust methodological finding; the empirical HS advantage is conditional on encoder quality and position control (partially correlated).\n2\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_013X3Sy2Z23u1dyGgpTwNcaf","session_id":"8f0c6665-94b6-4b25-a480-e39988e7cc27","uuid":"70743282-642c-4ad4-a671-91dcdf3b1cb9","timestamp":"2026-05-22T09:49:58.535Z"}
